WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.960
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:03.810
- [Narrator] This is Osetra Caviar

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:07.650
and all of this would cost $5,600.

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.570
The amount from these 16 tins

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:12.060
could come from a single Ossetra sturgeon,

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.160
which has to be raised
for at least seven years

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.590
before its eggs are ready for harvesting.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.840
- This fish is about 12 years old,

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.770
value approximately three to $4,000.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.230
- [Narrator] But to farm these
20,000 sturgeons sustainably

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:28.980
takes $500,000 a year in operating costs.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.740
From the feed, to the ultrasound checks,

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.140
to the very water that keeps them alive.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.550
- [Brian] Each one of
those drum filters you see

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.170
is about 30k.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.700
- [Narrator] We visited
Marshallberg Farm's

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.550
10 million facility in
Smyrna, North Carolina

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.433
to see how they produce
hundreds of kilograms of Caviar.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.240
One of the largest costs
of this whole operation

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.320
is the water

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.480
- Behind me are 12 grow out tanks.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.210
Each containing about
40,000 gallons of water

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.730
with about 300 fish apiece.

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:59.280
- [Narrator] These pipes are
the lifeline for the fish.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.710
They pump oxygenated water into the tanks

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.240
and pump back treated wastewater

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.160
to this part of the facility.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.400
The water goes through this
recirculating aquaculture system

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.430
or RAS for short and in one hour

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.290
the RAS system can filter through

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.810
a million gallons of water.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.270
A potential oversight
during a routine inspection

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.700
could lead to massive repercussions.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.320
- We've had our share of incidents here.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.710
I've come in and and found a tank

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.480
full of floating fish before.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:29.220
- [Narrator] So only after
everything is checked

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:31.923
can staff move on to
actually feeding the fish.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.160
The feed especially formulated

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.350
so it doesn't float or disintegrate

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.990
and it's distributed along
the perimeter of the tanks

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.230
so all 300 sturgeon have a
better chance of being fed.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:44.730
- We can feed 'em two pounds of feed

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:47.070
and expect roughly one pound of growth.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:50.490
Approximate cost of the feed
is about 75 cents a pound.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.500
The rough total for our facility per day

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.360
is about 400 pounds of feed.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.790
In any given month, we
expect 20 feed days.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:58.650
So you can do the math on roughly

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.860
how much it takes to operate
just this facility alone.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.370
- [Narrator] That math
adds up to $72,000 a year.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.620
- We have a significant economic reason

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.350
to go after and raise only females.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.620
Females of course go
for our Caviar operation

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.290
and males go to more
of our meat operation,

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:19.290
which is only about 5%
of our total revenue.

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.780
The challenge is you gotta
still raise the males

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.370
up to that five year point.

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.140
- [Narrator] That's
because up until five years

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.240
the fish can't be gender identified.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.400
- We have to look at
their gonadal structure

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:30.660
in order to determine that.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:34.050
And that's only large
enough when the fish is

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.070
of a certain age.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:36.690
- Once the females are identified,

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.250
they're separated from the males

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:39.840
and they live out their remaining days

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.540
and these tanks until
their eggs are ready.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.722
- Just to give you a
sense of scale, these fish

00:02:45.722 --> 00:02:50.722
I have from here in 2018,
they're now five years of age.

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:54.540
- [Narrator] The older the
fish, the larger the yield,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.130
and every quarter the farmers

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:59.400
carefully sort through each
of the 300 sturgeon by hand

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.550
to see if any are viable
for Caviar production.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.080
- Out of 300 fish in here,

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.510
we're expecting to be
able to harvest Caviar

00:03:06.510 --> 00:03:09.030
from 60 fish this season.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:12.030
These fish here, we got them in 2011,

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.640
so they're now going on 12 years of age.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.540
And these are prime candidate
fish for Caviar this year.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:20.190
- [Narrator] They keep their
interference with the fish

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:23.250
to a minimum in order to
reduce stress because.

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.470
- Stressed out fish don't make Caviar.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.801
You got a whopper, she
got something in her

00:03:27.801 --> 00:03:29.895
- Maybe little, softer.

00:03:29.895 --> 00:03:31.653
- It is a little softer.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.570
We do a palpation of the fish
on the belly to make sure

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:42.330
that there's something in
there for us to look at.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:43.740
Maybe, maybe, maybe.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.330
- [Narrator] If they
feel potential for eggs

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.033
in a fish's belly, they'll
perform an ultrasound.

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.990
- Okay, so what we're saying
here on the ultrasound,

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.270
what appears to be these
little white splotches

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.700
which are individual eggs,

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.090
we can get a profile view
of the entire diameter

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:04.170
and roughly project yields
based on what we see here.

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:05.520
- [Narrator] A biopsy will help them

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.470
test a sample of the row.

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:09.210
- I think we found a good Caviar fish.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:11.810
Take a peek this, we think
this one looks all right.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.600
So you can see the eggs
stay in the channel

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:16.620
of the biopsy tool.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.560
We can assess the firmness
of the egg, color texture,

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.800
and make a prediction that
this fish will yield a row

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.203
that we can make into

00:04:24.203 --> 00:04:25.593
- Is that really good pop?

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:27.720
- a quality Caviar.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:30.360
So this is not Caviar,
this is row at this point.

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:33.630
Only after salting and
packing does it become Caviar.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.340
- [Narrator] Eggs change
in texture and size

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:36.450
during each cycle.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.550
So if farmers miss the window to harvest,

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.170
they have to wait at least a year

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:43.290
to see if that fish has new
eggs with Caviar potential.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.720
- If it's really squishy,
it's gone past the time

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.170
when we should be harvesting it.

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:48.900
Cause if we don't do this step

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:50.970
we're gonna kill a fish
that could potentially

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:53.910
yield us great Caviar
later on down the road.

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:57.060
But if we don't get good Caviar
now, it has no market value.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:58.860
So this is very critical.

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:00.270
- [Narrator] Harvesting occurs when a fish

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:02.790
is at least seven to eight years old.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:06.390
The sturgeons are euthanized
before their row is extracted.

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:07.890
- Then we'll screen that row sack,

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:10.260
then we will salt the row

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:11.970
and finally we'll place it on a screen

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:14.250
where we'll pick through
it with a pair of tweezers,

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.080
any form matter out of there,

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.170
or little blood clots or
imperfectly shaped eggs.

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:21.900
We'll pick through that one by one by one.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.150
And this takes about an hour long process

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:26.190
before we pack it into a tin,

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:29.190
vacuum sealant, and then
cold store that tin.

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:31.680
- So this is where we
store all the Caviar.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:35.070
It's organized by grade and the tin size.

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:36.420
- [Narrator] Every harvest is labeled

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:37.860
with a designated lot number

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.660
which traces back to one fish

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:42.270
and a small sample is set
aside for each lot number

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:44.160
during the harvesting process.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:45.210
- With Ossetra, you know,

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:48.480
it's part of the grading system is color,

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.990
Ossetra Caviar has a tendency
to have a golden undertone,

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:53.580
and like the more golden it is,

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:56.670
it can be considered a higher grade.

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:58.500
- [Narrator] Marshallberg
Farm primarily produces

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:03.500
three major grades of Caviar,
every day, classic, and royal,

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:06.810
and occasionally some rare
grades like this gold reserve.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:11.810
- The grading is based on
appearance, texture, and flavor.

00:06:12.210 --> 00:06:15.330
And flavor is just, it's
just almost instinct,

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:17.760
it is a really buttery, savory,

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.400
you know, some just tastes
a lot richer than others.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:21.233
- [Narrator] Lianne can grade

00:06:21.233 --> 00:06:24.180
as many as 60 lot numbers in
a day during the busy season,

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:27.090
and the process can take
up to four hours a day.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:29.660
- I'm gonna use my hand
because it has no flavor

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:32.913
and my brain will not
associate any flavors with it.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:39.270
It's got a nutty, buttery
flavor, got some pop to it.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:41.550
So I would call this a classic grain.

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:42.383
- [Narrator] Marshallberg farm

00:06:42.383 --> 00:06:44.400
started their operation in 2011

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:46.560
with about 5,000 fingerlings.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:48.960
Now they have 20,000 sturgeon

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:50.760
with a projected Caviar production

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:53.430
of up to 1500 kilograms this year.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:56.640
That's more than double what
they produced last year.

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:01.290
The US imported $17.8 million
worth of Caviar in 2018

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:03.510
with half of those
imports coming from China.

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:06.840
But in recent years, interest
in domestic Caviar has grown.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:09.510
- But we're getting to the
point now where our demand

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:12.090
and sales outstrip our operating costs.

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:13.170
- [Narrator] And their operating costs

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:15.600
now include efforts to
spawn their crop on site

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:18.210
growing their next generation of sturgeon.

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:21.510
- We have an imperative to
complete the loop here ourselves,

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:24.660
but knowing that we have
quite a significant inventory

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:27.480
so that affords us the luxury of some time

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:28.710
to get spawning right.

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:31.076
In this tank we keep brood stock fish

00:07:31.076 --> 00:07:33.480
in the hopes of spawning them.

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:35.460
We hope that they just
do their job naturally

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:37.680
and we come out here one day and get lucky

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:41.941
and find a bunch of fertilized
eggs and always have a dream.

00:07:41.941 --> 00:07:45.191
(bright upbeat music)

